==Indonesian==
{{wikipedia||lang=id}}

===Etymology===
From {{bor|id|en|jockey}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|id|[ˈd͡ʒoki]}}
* {{hyphenation|id|jo|ki}}

===Noun===
{{head|id|noun|head=|}}

# {{l|en|jockey}}, one who rides racehorses competitively.
# {{lb|id|figurative}} {{l|en|impersonator}},
## one who asked to do {{l|en|impersonation}}, a form of cheating whereby a different person than the student assigned an assignment or exam completes it.
## [[w:Car jockey|car jockey]], one who asked to {{l|en|impersonate}} as a {{l|en|passenger}} in a random commuter who does not have enough passengers to legally use a carpool lane.
# {{l|en|disc jockey}}.

====Usage notes====
Car jockey was a way to circumvent {{w|Tiga dalam satu|lang=id|''kawasan pembatasan penumpang''}} (passenger restriction area) rule which mandated at least three person in a car (three-in-one) to legally use a line during specified time periods, i.e. {{m|en|rush hour}}/{{m|en|peak hour}}, based on {{w|high-occupancy vehicle lane}} concept. The rule was enforced from December 2003 to May 2016 at several roads in Jakarta.

